---
import ProjectDetail from '../../layouts/ProjectDetail.astro';
---

<ProjectDetail
  title="Housing Price Regression"
  description="Beginner-friendly multiple linear regression on the Kaggle Housing dataset, from outlier removal and RFE feature selection to OLS and a VIF check."
  tags={['ML', 'Linear Regression', 'Data Science']}
  github="https://github.com/MariamKhubashvilii/Housing-Price-Regression"
  accent="blue"
>
  <section class="project-grid">
    <article class="project-card summary-card">
      <h2>Project summary</h2>
      <p>
        A beginner-friendly notebook that predicts house prices with multiple linear regression on the classic Kaggle Housing dataset. The goal is not the most accurate model. It is to walk through a full regression workflow step by step: cleaning, encoding, picking features, fitting, and checking the result.
      </p>
      <div class="summary-list">
        <div>
          <strong>Clean before fitting</strong>
          <p>Uses boxplots to spot outliers in <code>price</code> and <code>area</code>, then removes them with the 1.5 × IQR rule.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Statistics, not just a score</strong>
          <p>Fits the final model with <code>statsmodels</code> OLS to read coefficients and p-values, and checks multicollinearity with VIF.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="project-card detail-card">
      <h2>What this project shows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Exploratory analysis with boxplots and a pairplot.</li>
        <li>IQR-based outlier removal.</li>
        <li>Binary and dummy encoding of categorical columns.</li>
        <li>RFE feature selection, OLS regression, and a VIF check.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The dataset has one row per house: price, area, bedrooms, bathrooms, stories, parking, yes/no amenities like a main road, guest room, basement, hot water heating, air conditioning and preferred area, and a furnishing status. The notebook maps the yes/no columns to 1/0, turns furnishing status into dummy columns, splits the data 70/30, scales the numeric columns with <code>MinMaxScaler</code>, and uses RFE to keep six features before fitting OLS. It then checks the residuals and scores the model with R² on the held-out test set (about 0.58).
      </p>

      <div class="section-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Steps covered</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>EDA with boxplots</li>
            <li>IQR outlier removal</li>
            <li>Binary and dummy encoding</li>
            <li>RFE feature selection</li>
            <li>OLS via statsmodels</li>
            <li>VIF check and test-set R²</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Features kept by RFE</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><code>area</code>, <code>bathrooms</code>, <code>stories</code></li>
            <li><code>airconditioning</code>, <code>parking</code>, <code>prefarea</code></li>
            <li><code>price</code> (target)</li>
            <li>Dataset: Kaggle Housing dataset</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section project-usage">
    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <p>
      Clone the repo, download the <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/yasserh/housing-prices-dataset" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kaggle Housing dataset</a>, point the <code>read_csv</code> path in the notebook at the file, and run the cells top to bottom.
    </p>
    <div class="usage-card">
      <div>
        <strong>Focus</strong>
        <p>Understanding a complete regression workflow, not building a production price predictor.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Best for</strong>
        <p>Students and self-learners who want to see each preprocessing and modeling step in order.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectDetail>
